    "faqs_nad_explained": {
      "type": "custom-faqs",
      "blocks": {
        "faq_q46": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What is NAD+?",
            "content": "<p>NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) is a coenzyme found in every cell, which cells use to turn food into usable energy and to run maintenance processes such as DNA repair. Your body makes it continuously from precursors like vitamin B3 and nicotinamide riboside, and recycles it as it’s used.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q47": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Does NAD+ decline with age?",
            "content": "<p>Research suggests NAD+ levels in some tissues fall with age, as demand rises and production and recycling slow. The size of the decline varies between people and tissues, and blood levels don’t track it neatly, so treat any single headline figure with caution.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q48": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What’s the difference between NAD+ and NADH?",
            "content": "<p>They’re two forms of the same molecule. When your cells break down food, NAD+ picks up electrons and becomes NADH. NADH then hands them on in your mitochondria to make energy, turning back into NAD+. The balance between the two is part of how your cells manage energy.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q49": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How do NAD+ supplements work?",
            "content": "<p>Most NAD+ supplements provide precursors such as NR, which your body converts into NAD+, rather than NAD+ itself, which is poorly absorbed whole. Oral NR at 300mg a day has been shown to raise blood NAD+ in human studies.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q50": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Can you raise NAD+ naturally?",
            "content": "<p>Regular exercise, enough vitamin B3 in your diet (meat, fish, wholegrains), good sleep and not overdoing alcohol all support how your body makes and recycles NAD+. Precursors like NR are a more direct route. MUTO is designed to sit alongside those habits, not replace them.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q51": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What are sirtuins?",
            "content": "<p>Sirtuins are a family of enzymes involved in cell maintenance, including DNA repair and energy regulation. They need NAD+ to work, which is one reason NAD+ is studied in ageing research.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q52": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What is HRV?",
            "content": "<p>Heart rate variability (HRV) is the variation in time between heartbeats, and a widely used signal of how your nervous system is handling stress and recovery. It’s personal, so never compare yours with someone else’s. It’s also noisy night to night, so the 30-day trend against your own baseline is what’s worth watching.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q53": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What’s the difference between healthspan and lifespan?",
            "content": "<p>Lifespan is how long you live. Healthspan is how long you stay healthy, capable and independent. MUTO is about healthspan: keeping your standard for as long as you can.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q54": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Does caffeine raise NAD+?",
            "content": "<p>Caffeine doesn’t raise NAD+. It blocks adenosine, the chemical signal behind sleepiness, so you feel more alert without changing how your cells make energy. That’s why the effect fades and resets each day.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
